  "top_assumptions": [
    "Credit growth recovers to 10-12% YoY post-pandemic as economic activity normalises, driving interest income higher, while investment income normalises from elevated FY2021 levels, yielding blended Total Income CAGR of ~9% over FY2022-24.",
    "Net Profit margin expansion continues as NPA provisioning requirements decline sharply from the elevated FY2020-21 cycle (Gross NPA fell from ~\u20b91,49,092 cr to ~\u20b91,26,389 cr), with credit costs normalising and deferred tax credit tailwinds partially reversing, supporting PAT CAGR of ~19% over FY2022-24."
  ],
  "reasoning": "For SBI (a bank/NBFC), Total Income = Interest Earned + Other Income is used as the Revenue line. FY2021 consolidated Total Income = \u20b9278,115 cr (Interest Earned) + \u20b9107,222 cr (Other Income) = \u20b9385,337 cr; FY2020 = \u20b9269,852 cr + \u20b998,159 cr = \u20b9368,011 cr, implying ~4.7% YoY growth \u2014 however the standalone segment data shows Total Income of ~\u20b9308,647 cr (FY21) vs \u20b9302,545 cr (FY20), roughly 2% growth per the Directors' Report. Using the consolidated figures from schedules 13 and 14, FY2021 Total Income is ~\u20b9385,337 cr vs FY2020 ~\u20b9368,011 cr (~4.7% YoY). FY2021 is anomalous: interest income on advances fell (loan growth was muted in COVID year, rate cuts compressed yields), while investment income surged on bond gains; NPA provisioning remained very high (~\u20b929,759 cr standalone). The anomaly flag is set because the revenue growth was suppressed and profit growth was distorted by one-off deferred tax credits (~\u20b93,749 cr) and lower-than-normal credit costs versus FY2020. Using a 3-year CAGR basis is not fully possible from the text (only two years of P&L data provided), so I use the ~4-5% revenue CAGR implied and adjust upward to ~9% for FY2022 onward as credit demand recovers post-pandemic, interest rates normalise/rise (RBI tightening cycle expected), and SBI's large deposit franchise re-prices. Net profit trajectory is anchored by declining provisioning drag and improving RoA (0.48% in FY2021, trending toward 0.6-0.65%), driving PAT from \u20b920,410 cr to ~\u20b934,000 cr by FY2024. EPS is computed on the stable share count of 892.46 crore shares."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "SBIN",
  "base_year": 2021,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Credit growth recovers to 10-12% YoY post-pandemic, driving blended Total Income CAGR of ~9% over FY2022-24, with rising interest rates benefiting SBI's large deposit franchise re-pricing.",
      "failure_scenario": "A third COVID wave or prolonged global supply-chain disruption suppresses private capex and retail credit demand into FY2023; simultaneously, aggressive RBI rate hikes compress NIMs as deposit re-pricing outpaces lending rate transmission, keeping revenue growth closer to 5-6% and making the ~9% CAGR unachievable.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "NPA provisioning requirements decline sharply from elevated FY2020-21 cycle levels, with Gross NPA falling further and credit costs normalising, driving PAT CAGR of ~19% over FY2022-24.",
      "failure_scenario": "Stress in MSME, agriculture, or mid-corporate segments resurfaces \u2014 particularly given SBI's large restructured book and COVID-related Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS) exposures \u2014 causing slippages to spike in FY2023-24, forcing higher-than-modelled provisions and compressing PAT well below the \u20b934,000 cr FY2024 target.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "EPS is computed on a stable share count of 892.46 crore shares throughout the forecast period, implying no material equity dilution.",
      "failure_scenario": "SBI undertakes a follow-on public offering or rights issue to bolster CET-1 capital ratios ahead of Basel IV norms or to fund balance-sheet expansion, meaningfully expanding the share count and diluting EPS below forecasted levels even if PAT targets are broadly met.",
      "risk": "Low"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 42,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast's PAT CAGR of ~19% is highly sensitive to the NPA provisioning assumption, which rests on a benign credit cycle that is far from guaranteed given SBI's historically volatile asset quality and its outsized exposure to restructured and ECLGS accounts. Furthermore, the revenue base used (consolidated \u20b9385,337 cr) inflates the starting point relative to standalone figures cited in the Directors' Report, creating an internal inconsistency that undermines confidence in the absolute forecast numbers."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
